---
import Base from "../../layouts/Base.astro";
import { projects } from "../../data/projects";

// Rewritten 1 October 2026 (XC): the project was narrowed to the comparison on human data.
// Earlier diagnostic runs on pretrained models are not part of it. Nothing has been fitted yet,
// so claim no result and no winner; keep it separate from the thesis.
const project = projects.modalModels;
---

<Base
  title={`${project.title} · Xiaochuan Li`}
  description="Three competing accounts of how evidence decides between must and might, compared on published human data."
  showProgress={true}
  toc={true}
>
  <article class="prose">
    <p class="!mb-6 !text-sm"><a href="/research#work-lm" class="!text-[var(--color-accent)]">← Research & Papers</a></p>
    <div class="text-xs uppercase tracking-wider text-[var(--color-text-dim)] font-medium mb-2">{project.status}</div>
    <h1>{project.title}</h1>
    <p class="text-[var(--color-text-muted)] italic !text-lg !mb-3">{project.question}</p>
    <p>{project.summary} {project.connection}</p>

    <h2>The finding</h2>
    <p>
      Which modal a speaker picks depends on more than how likely the proposition is. In the
      production data reported by Degen and colleagues (2019), people choosing among a bare
      assertion, <i>must p</i>, <i>probably p</i> and <i>might p</i> are sensitive both to how
      strong the evidence is and to whether it was obtained directly or by inference. Those
      two factors do not act alike: the source matters for <i>must</i> in a way it does not for
      <i>might</i>.
    </p>

    <h2>Three accounts</h2>
    <p>
      The pattern has more than one explanation, and they disagree about what <i>must</i> means.
      On the first, only evidence strength matters and the apparent effect of source is a
      residue of imperfectly matched strength. On the second, the requirement for indirect
      evidence is part of the meaning of <i>must</i>. On the third, it is not: <i>must</i>
      carries no such condition, and the effect arises because a bare assertion is the better
      thing to say when the evidence is direct, so the source acts on <i>must</i> only through
      that competition.
    </p>

    <h2>What a comparison can settle</h2>
    <p>
      Stated as probabilistic choice models over the four expressions, the three accounts can be
      fitted to the published trial-level data and compared with model selection criteria. Two
      checks decide how much the comparison is worth: whether the accounts can be told apart at
      all in this design — assessed by fitting each to data the others generated — and whether
      the winner still predicts conditions held out of the fit. If they cannot be told apart,
      that is the result, and the question becomes what materials would separate them. The
      original authors note that their data do not adjudicate between the two families of
      theory; a quantitative comparison is the step after that observation.
    </p>

    <h2>Language models as a second kind of user</h2>
    <p>
      The same accounts can be fitted to the choices a language model makes over the same four
      expressions. That asks whether the same account describes a system with a different
      history, not whether the model resembles a person. A further step, which the behavioural
      comparison cannot reach in people, is whether strength and source are represented
      separately inside the model, so that intervening on one changes <i>must</i> and leaves
      <i>might</i> alone. These come after the comparison on human data, and only if that
      comparison separates the accounts.
    </p>

    <h2>Status</h2>
    <p class="cw-status">
      Not started. The data and analysis scripts are public; the first step is to reproduce the
      reported analysis before anything is fitted. No account is favoured here, and no result is
      claimed. This project is separate from the master's thesis.
    </p>
    <p class="!mt-10 !text-sm !text-[var(--color-text-dim)]"><a href="/research">← Back to Research & Projects</a></p>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .cw-status { font-size: 0.98rem; line-height: 1.7; color: var(--color-text); padding: 1rem 1.2rem; border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent); background: var(--color-surface); border-radius: 0 8px 8px 0; }
</style>
